    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1163">

                <pb facs="1200" n="1188" sameAs="frus:frus1945v08/pg1188"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">890D.01/12–1845: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the
                        Ambassador in France</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Caffery</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1946-01-05T18:00:00-05:00">January 5, 1946—6
                            p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>60. If favorable opportunity presents itself you may wish to inform M. Bidault or
                    some other appropriate Foreign Ministry official that Dept’s views re
                    Anglo-French Levant agreement are those contained in first three paragraphs of
                    Dept’s 5975, Dec. 20, 9 p.m.<anchor n="93a" xml:id="d1163-f1"/> As stated in
                    Dept’s 6069, Dec. 28, 7[<hi rendition="#italic">8</hi>] p.m.,<anchor n="94"
                        xml:id="d1163-f2"/> these views were made clear, at Mr. Bevin’s request, to
                    Brit Govt which has indicated that substance thereof was communicated to
                    French.</p>
                <p>Oral communication received from Brit Emb here quotes Mr. Bevin as stating in
                    fact that our views enabled Brit “to represent very <pb facs="1201" n="1189"
                        xml:id="pg1189"/> forcibly to French Govt objections which would be felt in
                    America to the phrase ‘the leading role’ (contained in first draft agreement
                    shown Dept) and as a result we were able eventually to induce French Govt to
                    agree to revised wording which is not open to similar objection”.</p>
                <p>Sent Paris. Repeated London and Beirut for Damascus.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Byrnes</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
